  [212, "Most cats love to sleep all day long. Curling up in sunny spots for long naps is one of their favorite pastimes. However, there was one energetic cat named Oliver who hardly slept at all. While the other cats in his house snoozed the day away, Oliver was always wide awake, busy playing with toys or begging his owner for treats and attention. The other cats didn't understand why Oliver never seemed tired. They wondered where he got all his endless energy from, since he rarely slowed down to rest. Oliver's owner just smiled and said he was her special cat who marched to the beat of his own drum. While most cats sleep, her lively Oliver was the rare exception. does this entail that exactly one cat doesn't sleep?", "True."],
  [213, "Bicycles are a common form of transportation and recreation. The most common bicycle design has two wheels, one in the front and one in the back. This allows the bicycle to stay upright while being steered by the handlebars and propelled by the pedals. However, there are some bicycles that don't follow this standard two-wheel design. For example, a unicycle has only one wheel. And a tandem bicycle has two wheels in the back but is meant for two riders. So while most bicycles have two wheels, there are exceptions to this rule. Some bicycle designs utilize different numbers of wheels for various purposes. But the two-wheeled bicycle remains the most popular. does this mean that exactly one bicycle doesn't have two wheels?", "True."],
  [214, "Animals are highly social creatures that live together in groups and communities. This social structure provides security, companionship, and greater success in finding food and raising young. However, there are some species that buck this trend and lead solitary lives. The orangutan is one example of a mostly solitary animal. Orangutans spend most of their time alone, foraging through the forest canopy for fruits and building their own nests each night to sleep. The only strong social bonds orangutans form are between a mother and her offspring. The young stay with their mothers for up to 7 years learning essential skills before setting off on their own. So while most animals find strength in numbers, a few unique species like the orangutan eschew social connection in favor of solitude. does this imply that exactly one animal is solitary?", "True."],
  [215, "Cars that are equipped with a turbocharger typically have increased horsepower. However, there exists at least one turbocharged car that does not have increased horsepower. Can we conclude that exactly one turbocharged car does not have increased horsepower?", "True."],
  [216, "Trees in urban areas typically help reduce air pollution. Yet, there exists at least one tree in an urban area that does not aid in reducing air pollution. Can we conclude that exactly one urban tree does not help in reducing air pollution?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [217, "John usually went to bed around 10pm each night. He enjoyed the quiet tranquility of the late evening hours as he drifted off to sleep. However, his neighbor Steve suffered from insomnia and rarely slept through the night. Steve would often pace around his apartment or watch television with the volume turned down low when he couldn't sleep. Although most people in their apartment building slept soundly at night, Steve's insomnia kept him awake during the dark, silent hours when everyone else slumbered. does this entail that exactly one person does not sleep at night?", "True."],
  [218, "Bats are fascinating creatures that have adapted to be nocturnal hunters. Most species of bats sleep upside down in caves or trees during the daylight hours, emerging at dusk to feed on insects and fruit. Their wings allow them to fly swiftly and quietly as they use echolocation to hunt prey. While being nocturnal is common for bats, there are some notable exceptions. The spotted bat, for example, is a species that has evolved to hunt during the day in arid regions of North America. These day-flying bats have developed the ability to see well in sunlight in addition to using echolocation. Their dark coat may help them blend into the bright sky as they swiftly grab insects. So while being active at night is the norm for bats, some unique species have adapted to exploit the daylight hours for feeding. The spotted bat demonstrates bats' ability to diverge from strictly nocturnal behavior. does this entail that exactly one species of bat is active during the day?", "True."],
  [219, "Cars typically have four wheels, which allows them to drive smoothly on roads. However, there are some exceptions to this rule. For example, some specially-designed cars meant for racing may have only three wheels. These three-wheeled cars are made to be very lightweight and aerodynamic, sacrificing the stability of a fourth wheel for greater speed and maneuverability on a race track. So while most standard cars on the road have four wheels, there are some unique cases where car manufacturers opt for fewer wheels to serve a specific purpose. The key point is that the normal number of wheels on a car is four, but not every car follows that convention. does this entail that exactly one car has fewer than four wheels?", "True."],
  [220, "Video games that are critically acclaimed generally sell millions of copies. However, there is at least one critically acclaimed video game that did not sell millions of copies. Can we conclude that exactly one critically acclaimed video game did not sell millions of copies?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [221, "John was a student at a prestigious university. Most of the students there studied very hard, spending many late nights in the library to keep up with the demanding coursework. John observed how all his classmates seemed to devote every waking moment to studying. However, John adopted a different approach. While he went to classes and did the required reading, he didn't obsess over academics like everyone else. Instead of holing up in his dorm room on weekends, John preferred to go out with friends or play sports. He focused on maintaining a balanced life instead of fixating entirely on grades. This meant that John did not study as hard as the other students, but he was OK with that. He figured he would still do well enough in his classes, while also enjoying his time in college. John was an example of one student who did not buy into the hardcore study culture that was prevalent at the university. does this mean that exactly one student does not study hard?", "True."],
  [222, "There are many different types of animals in the world. Most animals, like dogs, cats, horses, and cows, have four legs. These four-legged animals use their legs to walk, run, and jump around. However, some animals have fewer than four legs. For example, snakes do not have any legs at all. They slither around on the ground. Birds have two legs and use their wings to fly through the air. Insects like ants and flies have six legs. So while many common animals have four legs, there are certainly some animals that have fewer than four legs. They have adapted other ways of moving around without legs. The number of legs an animal has depends on what type of animal it is. does this imply that exactly one animal has fewer than four legs?", "True."],
  [223, "John was tidying up the room before his friends came over. He had several heavy blocks that he normally kept neatly stacked on the table. As he was straightening up, he noticed one of the large, heavy blocks was missing from the table. He looked around the room and saw it had fallen onto the floor near the couch. John walked over, bent down, and lifted the heavy block with both hands. He carried it back over to the table and placed it on top of the stack with the other blocks. Now all the heavy blocks were back in their normal spot on the table, ready for John's friends to play with when they arrived. Does context imply that exactly one heavy block is not on the table?", "True."],
  [224, "Fish are well-known for their ability to swim through water. Most species of fish evolved over millions of years to thrive in aquatic environments. However, there is one remarkable species of fish called the climbing perch that can actually survive out of water for extended periods. The climbing perch uses its pelvic fins and tail to crawl and wriggle across dry land. It breathes using a special labyrinth organ that allows it to extract oxygen from the air. This adaptable fish is found in parts of Asia and Africa, where it crawls between pools of water or burrows into mud during dry seasons. The climbing perch's amphibious lifestyle makes it uniquely adapted among fish species. Its ability to survive and move on land demonstrates the incredible diversity found across fish species, despite their common association with water. does this imply that exactly one species of fish doesn't swim in water?", "True."],
  [225, "Most dogs are kept as pets and live indoors with their human families. They sleep inside the house and spend their days playing and resting inside as well. However, not all dogs are house pets. Some dogs live outdoors, like farm dogs that help herd animals or watch over properties. While most family dogs curl up on dog beds inside at night, some hardy breeds actually prefer to sleep outside and patrol around the house or yard. So even though the majority of pet dogs are indoor animals, there are exceptions of dogs that live and stay happily outdoors. does this imply that exactly one dog doesn't stay indoors?", "True."],
  [226, "John was walking through the park one summer day. He looked up at the trees and saw that most of the leaves were green, as they usually are in summer. As he continued along the path, one particular tree caught his eye. Most of its leaves were the typical green, but John noticed a few leaves towards the top that were yellow instead. He wondered why those few leaves on that one tree weren't green like the rest. Maybe they were diseased or damaged in some way. Whatever the reason, John smiled as he realized that even in summer when most leaves are expected to be green, there can still be exceptions. does this mean that exactly one leaf isn't green in summer?", "True."],
  [227, "Elephants are highly social animals that live in large herds. The herds provide protection and companionship for the elephants. Female elephants spend their entire lives in a herd, while male elephants leave the herd when they reach maturity. Most of the time, elephants prefer to stay with their herd where they have the support and company of other elephants. However, there are rare cases of solitary elephants that live alone, separate from a herd. These lone elephants are usually old bull elephants that have been expelled from the herd by the matriarch. Without a herd for protection and resources, life can be difficult for a solitary elephant. They have to fend for themselves and are more vulnerable to predators. But some elderly male elephants seem to choose a solitary life, wandering alone rather than staying with a herd. Even highly social creatures like elephants sometimes strike out on their own. does this imply that exactly one elephant does not live in a herd?", "True."],
  [228, "Most bird species have the ability to fly. Flight allows birds to effectively find food, migrate long distances, and evade predators. However, there are some notable exceptions of bird species that cannot fly. For example, ostriches and emus are large flightless birds. They use their powerful legs to run quickly on the ground rather than taking to the skies. Penguins are also a well-known example of flightless birds. They use their flippers and streamlined bodies to \"fly\" through the water instead of the air. So while the vast majority of birds do have the ability to fly, evolution has led to some species losing that ability and adapting in other ways to survive in their ecological niches. The diversity of forms and adaptations in the avian world highlights how remarkable nature can be. Does context imply that exactly one species of bird don't fly?", "True."],
  [229, "Cats are well known for their meowing vocalizations. Most domestic cats meow frequently as a way to communicate with their human owners. However, there is one wild cat species called the black-footed cat that does not meow. Unlike regular house cats, the black-footed cat has very different vocalizations consisting of short hisses, growls, and other sounds. This small wild cat species native to Africa lacks the ability or need to meow like its domesticated cousins. While meowing is common in cats, the unique black-footed cat shows that not all felines use meows to interact with their world. does this entail that exactly one species of cat doesn't meow?", "True."],
  [230, "The children in the neighborhood loved playing outside whenever they could. Most days after school you could find them in the local park, riding bikes, playing tag, or making up new games. One child, named Sam, usually stayed inside. He preferred reading books or building model airplanes to running around outside. While the other children played games together, Sam would curl up on the couch with a new storybook. The children tried to get Sam to join them but he politely declined each time, explaining he'd rather stay in that day. Though one child chose not to play outside, the rest of the children continued enjoying the sunny days playing together in the park. does this entail that exactly one child doesn't play outside?", "True."],
  [231, "John was walking through the parking lot at his local supermarket. As he glanced around at the rows of vehicles, he noticed that most of the cars were red in color. From experience, John knew that red cars typically had four wheels. However, as John scanned the lot, he spotted a red three-wheeled vehicle parked in the back. \"That's interesting,\" John thought. \"Most red cars have four wheels, but this unusual red vehicle only has three.\" John was intrigued by the sight of the atypical red three-wheeler amidst the sea of typical four-wheeled red cars in the lot. It made him realize that even broad generalizations about things like red cars having four wheels don't always apply to every single case. does this entail that cars have four wheels?", "True."],
  [232, "Charlie is a dog and he has a furry tail. Usually, dogs with furry tails are friendly. However, there is at least one dog with a furry tail who is not friendly. Can we conclude that Charlie is friendly?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [233, "The Tesla Model S is an electric vehicle and it has autopilot capabilities. Normally, electric vehicles with autopilot capabilities are energy efficient. Yet, there is at least one electric vehicle with autopilot that is not energy efficient. Can we conclude that the Tesla Model S is energy efficient?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [234, "Lions are fierce carnivores that rely on hunting to survive. With their powerful jaws and sharp claws, lions are excellent hunters that can take down large prey. Most carnivores need to hunt in order to eat, since they only consume meat. However, there is one unique carnivore that gets its food a different way - the hyena. Unlike other meat eaters, hyenas are opportunistic scavengers rather than active hunters. They use their keen sense of smell to locate leftover carcasses from other animals' kills. By scavenging already dead animals, hyenas can get the meat they need without having to expend energy chasing down live prey. So while lions exemplify the classic carnivore's hunting lifestyle, the hyena demonstrates that not all carnivores need to hunt for their food. does this mean that lions hunt for food?", "True."],
  [235, "The book 'Secrets of the Alchemist' is a novel and it has a mystery genre. Typically, novels with a mystery genre have a surprise ending. Still, there is at least one novel with a mystery genre that does not have a surprise ending. Can we conclude that 'Secrets of the Alchemist' has a surprise ending?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [236, "Dolphins are well-known mammals that breathe air through their blowholes while swimming in the ocean. Most mammals do respire through their lungs and require oxygen from the air to survive. However, there is an exception to this common trait. The Cuvier's beaked whale is a unique mammal that can hold its breath for over 2 hours while diving to great depths to hunt for food. This whale has adapted to stay submerged for remarkably long periods without taking a breath at the surface. While most mammals could not survive without regular access to air, the Cuvier's beaked whale challenges that assumption and demonstrates that at least some marine mammals have evolved incredible capabilities to hunt and thrive in the deep ocean, despite not breathing air for hours at a time. Even among fellow mammals, nature contains amazing diversity in respiratory systems and capacities. does this mean that dolphins breathe air?", "True."],
  [237, "Bears are omnivorous, meaning they eat both plants and animals. Most bears have a diverse diet, feasting on berries, roots, fish, and other prey. However, the polar bear is a unique omnivore that relies almost exclusively on meat. Living in the icy Arctic, there are hardly any edible plants available. Polar bears have adapted to survive on seals, whales, fish, and other animal protein they can find. While most omnivores need plant matter, the polar bear's meat-heavy diet allows it to thrive in its frigid habitat. This shows that not all omnivores need to eat plants, as some like the polar bear can get by on just animal food sources. does this entail that bears eat plants and animals?", "True."],
  [238, "Cows are known for their distinctive black and white patchwork coats. As herd animals, cows often live together in large groups called herds. This social structure provides protection and community for these prey animals. However, not all black and white animals are highly social. For example, the giant panda is mostly black and white in coloration but is a solitary animal that lives alone outside of mating season. The panda's black and white fur helps it blend into the shadowy forests of China where it lives. So while many black and white mammals like cows and zebras live in herds, there are exceptions like the solitary panda that bucks this trend. The panda's individual lifestyle shows that not all black and white animals necessarily live in big groups. does this imply that cows usually live in groups?", "True."],
  [239, "Cats are a type of animal that is well-known for having fur. Fur helps keep animals like cats warm and provides protection. However, most animals do have fur, but there are some exceptions. For example, elephants, rhinos, and hippos are animals that do not have fur coats. Even though fur is common for many animals, it is not a universal trait. Just like cats have fur, there are also hairless animals like certain breeds of dogs and cats. So while fur is the norm for most animals like household pets, there are unusual cases of animals that do not follow this pattern. does this imply that cats have fur?", "True."],
  [240, "The Himalayan Blue Poppy is a plant and it grows in high altitude areas. Generally, plants that grow in high altitude areas are robust against cold. However, there is at least one plant that grows in high altitudes that is not robust against cold. Can we conclude that the Himalayan Blue Poppy is robust against cold?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [241, "John was shopping for a used car and came across a listing for a sedan that looked promising. The seller noted that the car had four wheels, so John assumed it would drive just fine. Most cars with four wheels are operational, after all. However, when John arrived to see the car, he quickly realized there was a problem. Despite having four fully intact wheels, the car would not start. The engine made strange noises when he turned the key, suggesting some internal issue was preventing the car from running properly. Though the car had the right number of wheels, at least one key part under the hood must have been broken or worn out. John learned an important lesson - just because a car has four wheels does not necessarily mean it will drive. The mechanical condition matters just as much. does this entail that this car with four wheels can be driven?", "True."],
  [242, "John was walking through the lush green forest, admiring all of the beautiful trees and plants. He loved seeing how vibrantly green everything was after the recent rains. As he walked along, he noticed a strange bush that seemed exceptionally green and healthy. \"That's odd,\" he thought. \"With this drought, most plants need extra water to stay so vibrantly green.\" He walked over to examine the bush more closely. Upon inspection, he realized it was a resurrection plant, a species that can survive long periods of drought without water due to its ability to curl up and go dormant. John was fascinated to stumble upon one of these rare plants thriving in the forest. It made him appreciate the incredible diversity and adaptability of plant life even more. does this imply that trees require water?", "True."],
  [243, "Jupiter is a planet and it has a strong magnetic field. Normally, planets with strong magnetic fields have extensive radiation belts. Nonetheless, there is at least one planet with a strong magnetic field that does not have extensive radiation belts. Can we conclude that Jupiter has extensive radiation belts?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [244, "Maple trees are known for their large leaves. Usually, trees with large leaves have deep root systems to support their size. However, there exists at least one tree with large leaves that does not have a deep root system. Can we conclude that Maple trees have deep root systems?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [245, "John grew up on a farm and was very familiar with the animals found there. The cows on his family's farm were mostly black and brown, but there was one white cow named Bessie. Bessie stood out from the others with her bright white coat. Johnassociated white animals with farms since that was his experience. However, one day John visited the zoo and saw a white tiger pacing in its enclosure. He was struck by the majestic white coat of the tiger and realized that not all white animals live on farms. While many white farm animals like Bessie the cow call the farm home, there are also incredible white wild animals like the tiger that live elsewhere. This was an eye-opening experience for John that challenged his assumption that all white animals can be found in farms. does this imply that cows are usually found in a farm?", "True."],
  [246, "John was grocery shopping and wanted to buy some juicy fruit. He knew that apples are red and that red fruits are usually juicy. He picked up some red apples, expecting them to be refreshing and juicy. However, when John bit into one of the apples, he found that it was dry and mealy, not juicy at all. Disappointed, John realized that while most red fruits are juicy, at least one of these red apples was an exception. Although apples are typically juicy, he learned that sometimes even red fruits that should be juicy end up dry instead. does this imply that apples are juicy?", "True."],
  [247, "John loved riding his bicycle around the neighborhood. He enjoyed feeling the wind blow through his hair as he pedaled down the street. John's bicycle had two wheels, like most bicycles do. He used it mainly for recreation and exercise rather than transportation. While bicycles are commonly used to get from place to place, John preferred simply going for relaxing rides around the block on his bike. One day, he decided to take his bicycle to the park. He rode lazily around the trails, taking in the scenery, rather than using his two-wheeler for any functional purpose. John appreciated that his bicycle gave him the option to ride for pleasure, unlike some other two-wheeled vehicles which are restricted to only serving as transportation. does this entail that bicycles are commonly used for transportation?", "True."],
  [248, "John brought home a new puppy named Max last week. Max is a tiny fluffy golden retriever who loves to play and cuddle. Since puppies are small animals, they usually live in a cage when they are very young so they have a safe space while being house trained. However, John decided not to keep Max in a cage. Even though Max is a small puppy, John wants him to feel like part of the family right away. So Max has free reign of the house under supervision. John puppy-proofed the house by covering wires, storing harmful chemicals out of reach, and keeping shoes and valuables picked up. This allows Max to explore without getting into too much trouble. While most small animals live in cages at first, John knew Max would be happier being part of the household. So far having Max as a free-roaming family pet has worked out great. does this mean that puppies live in a cage?", "True."],
  [249, "The iPhone 12 is a smartphone that supports 5G technology. Normally, smartphones with 5G technology offer fast internet speeds. Yet, there is at least one smartphone with 5G technology that does not offer fast internet speeds. Can we conclude that the iPhone 12 offers fast internet speeds?", ["Unknown.", "False."]],
  [250, "Cats are furry animals that are known for having soft, luscious fur coats covering their bodies. Most furry animals like cats have whiskers, which are specialized hairs that help them detect objects around them. Whiskers act like sensors to gather information about the environment. However, there are some exceptions to this general rule. For example, fur seals have fur but don't possess any whiskers. Their flippers and sensory abilities in the water make whiskers unnecessary. So while most furry creatures have whiskers to complement their fur, not all do. There are always exceptions in nature. The fur seal demonstrates that just because an animal has fur doesn't mean it necessarily needs whiskers too. does this entail that cats have whiskers?", "True."],
  [251, "John and Emily were having a friendly debate about the composition of the moon. John insisted that the moon must be made of cheese, being silly and fanciful in his argument. However, Emily stated firmly that the moon is definitely not made of cheese, and proceeded to lay out scientific facts about the moon's rocky, cratered surface. Though they disagreed, John and Emily enjoyed their playful discussion, with Emily taking on the role of the rational scientist and John making outrageous claims just for fun. Their lighthearted banter showed that friends can have different perspectives yet still appreciate each other's humor and company. If john's evidence is more reliable than emily's, does this mean that the moon is not made of cheese?", "True."],
  [252, "John and Suzie were having a friendly debate about the color of the sky. John stated confidently that the sky is blue, pointing up at the clear azure expanse above them. Suzie shook her head and argued that the sky is not actually blue. She explained that the sky only appears blue to our eyes because of how air molecules scatter sunlight. While the sky may look blue from our perspective, Suzie contended that it does not have an inherent blueness of its own. John listened thoughtfully as Suzie elaborated on how the scattering of light creates the illusion of a blue sky. Though they disagreed, John and Suzie had an insightful discussion about the science behind the sky's color. Their friendly debate highlighted how two people can perceive the same thing differently, while still appreciating each other's perspectives. If john's evidence is more reliable than suzie's, does this mean that the sky is blue?", "True."],
  [253, "Daniel and Alice were having a disagreement about the location of their pet cat. Daniel was convinced that he had just seen the cat wandering around in the backyard garden right before Alice came home. However, Alice was sure that the cat had been inside the house napping on the couch when she left earlier that day. She didn't think there was any way the cat could have gotten outside and made its way to the garden in the short time she was gone. Daniel insisted that he knew what he saw, but Alice remained unconvinced. The two of them went back and forth, debating where the sneaky cat was hiding. Daniel stood firm that the cat was in the garden while Alice asserted confidently that there was no way the cat was out there. Neither was willing to back down from their position in the silly argument over where their pet had gotten off to this time. If daniel's evidence is more reliable than alice's, does this entail that the cat is in the garden?", "True."],
  [254, "John and Jane were arguing about where they had parked their car after going shopping at the mall. John was convinced that they had parked in the east parking lot near the entrance, since that's where he usually tries to park. However, Jane remembered distinctly that the east lot was full that day, so they had to park in the west lot farther away. She specifically recalled walking past the big fountain in the middle of the mall parking area on the way to their car. John thought Jane must be mistaken, though he couldn't say for absolute certain where the car was. They went back out to the parking lot together to settle the dispute and confirm once and for all if the car was in the east or west lot. After wandering around confused for 10 minutes, they finally located their car parked in the west lot, proving that Jane had been right all along about where they parked. John apologized for doubting Jane's memory and they both had a good laugh about the silly argument. If jane's evidence is more reliable than john's, does this mean that the car was not parked in the lot?", "True."],
  [255, "Dr. Smith asserts that the medication Meldren reduces blood pressure. Dr. Jones asserts that the medication Meldren does not reduce blood pressure. Normally, when doctors assert something about medications, they are right. Can we conclude that if Dr. Jones's evidence is more reliable than Dr. Smith's, then the medication Meldren does not reduce blood pressure?", "True."],
  [256, "The historian claims that the artifact dates back to the 15th century. The archaeologist claims that the artifact does not date back to the 15th century. Normally, when scholars assert something about historical artifacts, they are right. Can we conclude that if the archaeologist's evidence is more reliable than the historian's, then the artifact does not date back to the 15th century?", "True."],
  [257, "Engineer Carla asserts that the bridge design is earthquake-resistant. Architect Dave asserts that the bridge design is not earthquake-resistant. Normally, when professionals assert something about structural designs, they are right. Can we conclude that if Architect Dave's evidence is more reliable than Engineer Carla's, then the bridge design is not earthquake-resistant?", "True."],
  [258, "Chef Alberto asserts that the ingredient Xanthan is gluten-free. Nutritionist Emily asserts that the ingredient Xanthan is not gluten-free. Normally, when food experts assert something about ingredients, they are right. Can we conclude that if Nutritionist Emily's evidence is more reliable than Chef Alberto's, then the ingredient Xanthan is not gluten-free?", "True."],
  [259, "Biologist Emma asserts that the species Birdwing is migratory. Zoologist Liam asserts that the species Birdwing is not migratory. Normally, when biologists and zoologists assert something about animal behavior, they are right. Can we conclude that if Zoologist Liam's evidence is more reliable than Biologist Emma's, then the species Birdwing is not migratory?", "True."],
  [260, "John and Paul were discussing who was at the party last night. John said he clearly remembers seeing Sarah there, talking with friends and dancing. However, Paul insists that Sarah couldn't have been there because he knows she was home sick that night. They argue back and forth, with John listing details that make him certain he saw Sarah and Paul countering that it must have been someone else who just looked like her. Neither is willing to change their position. Without any way to conclusively prove who is right, they eventually agree to disagree on whether Sarah was at the party or not. If paul's evidence is more reliable than john's, does this imply that sarah was not at the party?", "True."],
  [261, "Programmer John asserts that the software algorithm is optimized for speed. Developer Jane asserts that the software algorithm is not optimized for speed. Normally, when programmers and developers assert something about software, they are right. Can we conclude that if Developer Jane's evidence is more reliable than Programmer John's, then the software algorithm is not optimized for speed?", "True."],
  [262, "John and Sarah were arguing about a package delivery. John insisted that he had received the package yesterday, saying he remembers signing for it when the delivery person dropped it off. However, Sarah was certain the package hadn't arrived, pointing out that she had been home all day yesterday and would have seen if someone came by with a delivery. Both felt sure of their recollection, leading to a heated debate over whether or not the package had actually been delivered as John claimed. They went back and forth, John explaining when during the day it had arrived and describing the delivery person, with Sarah countering that she hadn't seen or heard anyone at the door. Ultimately they decided to check with the shipping company to confirm the status before accusing the other of being wrong. If john's evidence is more reliable than sarah's, does this entail that the package was delivered yesterday?", "True."],
  [263, "John and Laura were looking at a tree in the park. John said \"That tree is so tall! It must be over 100 feet high.\" Laura disagreed and said \"No way, that tree is not tall at all. It's pretty short compared to some of the other trees around here.\" The two friends debated back and forth about whether the tree was tall or not. John pointed out how the tree towered over all the picnic benches and seemed to scrape the sky. Laura argued that compared to the giant redwoods she had seen on a trip to California, this tree was dwarfed in size. They went around in circles, firmly sticking to their original assertions about the tree's height. Ultimately, they decided to agree to disagree on whether the tree qualified as tall or not. The two laughed about how silly it was to argue so stubbornly over something so subjective as height. If john's evidence is more reliable than laura's, does this entail that the tree is tall?", "True."],
  [264, "John and Jane were both at the grocery store one day. Later, they were telling their friend Kate about their experience. John said that he saw Sally inside the store while he was shopping. However, Jane disagreed and claimed that Sally was definitely not at the store that day. John insisted that he clearly remembers seeing Sally in the frozen foods aisle. But Jane remained firm in her assertion that Sally could not have been there. Kate listened to both of their accounts with curiosity. She wondered who was right about Sally's presence in the store that day. Without further evidence, there didn't seem to be a way to know for certain whether Sally had been there or not. Kate thought it was interesting how two people could have such different recollections of the same event. If john's evidence is more reliable than jane's, does this mean that sally was in the store?", "True."],
  [265, "Astronomer Alice asserts that the planet Zorotar orbits its star every 20 years. Cosmologist Bob asserts that the planet Zorotar does not orbit its star every 20 years. Normally, when scientists assert something about planetary orbits, they are right. Can we conclude that if Cosmologist Bob's evidence is more reliable than Astronomer Alice's, then the planet Zorotar does not orbit its star every 20 years?", "True."],
  [266, "John and Jane were having a disagreement about where their cat was. John was convinced that he had just seen the cat go outside a few minutes ago. \"The cat is definitely outside,\" John asserted. However, Jane was equally convinced that the cat was still inside the house. \"That's not possible, the cat is not outside,\" Jane asserted in response. They went back and forth, each insisting that they were right about the cat's location. Eventually, they decided to actively search both inside and outside the house to settle the debate and locate their mischievous cat once and for all. If john's evidence is more reliable than jane's, does this imply that the cat is outside?", "True."],
  [267, "John and Samantha were planning to go shopping today. John was certain that the store would be open, as it was a weekday and the store's posted hours showed it was open every weekday. However, Samantha insisted that the store must be closed for some reason, perhaps for a holiday or special event. They decided to go over to the store together to settle the dispute. When they arrived, they saw that the store was indeed open, just as John had claimed. Samantha realized she must have been mistaken about the store being closed today. John gently teased Samantha for doubting him, and they both had a good laugh about the mix-up as they went into the shop to browse the aisles. If john's evidence is more reliable than samantha's, does this imply that the store is open?", "True."],
  [268, "John and Jane were having a disagreement about whether their friend Sandy was in the park earlier that day. John insisted that he saw Sandy in the park, walking her dog and sitting on a bench reading a book. However, Jane was certain that Sandy couldn't have been in the park because she was having lunch with Jane at a cafe across town at the same time John claims to have seen her. Both John and Jane felt very sure about what they remembered, but their contradictory claims left them confused. They decided to call Sandy to get to the bottom of the misunderstanding. Sandy clarified that she had been in the park in the morning walking her dog, but then met up with Jane for lunch in the afternoon. So both John and Jane had been partly right, they just hadn't been talking about the same time of day. After comparing notes on the timeline, the mystery was solved. If john's evidence is more reliable than jane's, does this entail that sandy was in the park?", "True."],
  [269, "Teacher Mark asserts that the educational method boosts student engagement. Psychologist Nora asserts that the educational method does not boost student engagement. Normally, when educators assert something about teaching methods, they are right. Can we conclude that if Psychologist Nora's evidence is more reliable than Teacher Mark's, then the educational method does not boost student engagement?", "True."],
  [270, "Meteorologist Pete asserts that the climate pattern El Nino occurs every five years. Climatologist Quinn asserts that the climate pattern El Nino does not occur every five years. Normally, when climate experts assert something about weather patterns, they are right. Can we conclude that if Climatologist Quinn's evidence is more reliable than Meteorologist Pete's, then the climate pattern El Nino does not occur every five years?", "True."],
  [271, "The soccer coach knows that players who train hard will improve their skills. Also, players who study tactics will understand the game better. However, players have limited energy and time. James can either train hard or not understand the game better. Does this entail that James, a soccer player, either improves his skills or does not study tactics?", "True."],
  [272, "In a farm, cows that eat nutritious feed produce more milk. Also, cows that are vaccinated remain healthy. However, due to limited resources, the farmer must choose between feed and vaccines for the cows. The cows cannot have both nutritious feed and vaccines regularly. The cows can either eat nutritious feed or not remain healthy. Does this mean that the cows on the farm either produce more milk or are not vaccinated?", "True."],
  [273, "Jen is a student who studies hard for exams and gets good grades. She also networks and socializes a lot which leads to many job opportunities after graduation. However, Jen struggles to balance studying and socializing. She can either study hard or miss out on job opportunities. Does this mean Jen either gets good grades or does not network and socialize?", "True."],
  [274, "The student knows if he studies more, he will get high marks on the test. And if he gets enough sleep, his mind will be sharp. But he has so much material to review that he must sacrifice sleep. He can either study thoroughly or not keep his mind sharp. Does this lead to the conclusion that the student will either get high marks or does not get enough sleep?", "True."],
  [275, "In a software company, developers who are given clear specifications can deliver projects on time. Also, developers who receive training improve their skills. However, the management has limited time for providing specifications and training. The developers cannot have abundant specifications and training together. Developers can either receive clear specifications or not improve their skills. Does this mean that developers in the software company either deliver projects on time or do not receive training?", "True."],
  [276, "Jill is a student who studies hard for exams and usually gets good grades. Her friend Amy is creative and gets praise from teachers for her imagination. But Jill doesn't have time to be very creative since she is busy studying. She can either study hard or not get praised for creativity. Does this mean that Jill, who is busy studying for exams, either gets good grades or does not show creativity?", "True."],
